<h1>HD Format v2 (Markdown-primary)</h1>

<p><strong>HD version 2</strong> stores document content as <strong>Markdown first, HTML only where Markdown can't express the intent</strong>. It is <strong>now the default</strong>: new documents are created as v2, and every save writes v2 — regardless of file extension. The older body-only-HTML format is version 1, still readable and migrated to v2 the first time you edit it. (The <code>.hd2</code> extension was the original home of this format and still opens; it is now a deprecated alias for a v2 <code>.hd</code> file.)</p>

<aside>
  <p><strong>Status: default format.</strong> Version 2 has replaced version 1 as the format new content is created and saved in. A legacy v1 (<code>.hd</code>, HTML) file opens unchanged and is migrated to v2 on its first edit — the <code>version:</code> frontmatter field, not the file extension, records which version a file is. There is no separate <code>.hd2</code> format to consolidate anymore; it is simply an alternate extension for the same v2 content.</p>
</aside>

<h2>The one-sentence difference</h2>

<p>Version 1 stores <strong>body-only HTML</strong> on disk; version 2 stores <strong>Markdown with raw-HTML islands</strong> on disk. Everything else — the editor, the frontmatter, the asset model, the document identity — is shared.</p>

<h2>What's identical to HD</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>The editor.</strong> The same VS Code WYSIWYG editor opens both. Typing, tables, paste, image handling, and the toolbar are unchanged.</li>
  <li><strong>Frontmatter.</strong> The same bare <code>---</code> YAML fence, the same fields (<code>id</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>date</code>, <code>version</code>, …).</li>
  <li><strong>Document identity.</strong> The same 22-character base62 <code>id</code>, injected on first open.</li>
  <li><strong>Assets.</strong> The same <code>.hd/&lt;id&gt;/</code> asset folders, keyed by <code>id</code>. HD2 deliberately reuses the existing asset directory so a document can move between flavors without touching its assets.</li>
  <li><strong>The editing capability set.</strong> Anything you can author in HD, you can author in HD2 — merged table cells, inline SVG, styled spans, and so on. The difference is only how those things are <em>serialized to disk</em>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What's different from HD</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>On-disk representation.</strong> Prose, headings, lists, simple tables, and other Markdown-expressible content are stored as Markdown. Everything Markdown can't represent is stored as a verbatim HTML island.</li>
  <li><strong>Frontmatter version.</strong> v2 files carry <code>version: 2</code>; legacy v1 files carry <code>version: 1</code>. The field is the source of truth for how the body is stored.</li>
  <li><strong>Cleaner diffs, more familiar source.</strong> The raw file reads like Markdown and produces smaller, line-oriented git diffs for prose edits.</li>
  <li><strong>A first-save reformat.</strong> Because the editor re-serializes to a single canonical Markdown style, the first save of a hand-written or imported file may reflow it once. See <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="how-it-works.hd">How HD2 Works</a>.</li>
</ul>

